"""Days 1–2: a careful container mental model and a reviewed Dockerfile."""
L(0,'1. Image, container, engine and registry','Explain what runs, what is packaged, and what remains outside a container.',
 [('Image','A layered, read-only package containing application files, dependencies and startup defaults. It is a recipe result, not a running process.'),('Container','A running or stopped instance of an image with isolated processes, networking and a writable layer. Linux containers share a kernel; they are not each a full virtual machine.'),('Docker Engine and registry','The engine builds/runs containers. A registry stores and distributes images. Docker Desktop on Windows normally supplies a Linux VM and engine for Linux containers.')],
 'Week 41 made API behaviour repeatable. Week 42 packages and operates that API. Begin with four separate nouns: source, image, container and data. Editing source does not change an existing image; rebuilding does not replace a running container until you recreate it. A registry stores an image but does not run your API.\n\nA container includes a Python interpreter and libraries, but its host still matters: CPU architecture, kernel support, memory, ports, credentials and persistent storage need a plan. A successful local Python command below proves only that this interpreter ran. The terminal exercise runs Python in an actual container; do not confuse those two pieces of evidence.',
 'import sys\nrelease={"source":"Notes API", "image":"studyhub-notes:42", "container":"notes-api", "data":"PostgreSQL volume"}\nfor name,value in release.items():print(name+":",value)\nprint("Local interpreter:",sys.implementation.name)',
 'Create a four-item inventory for a fictional photo API, keeping persistent uploads separate from its image.',
 'parts={"source":"photo-api", "image":"photo-api:42", "container":"photo-web", "data":"uploads volume"}\nprint("Image:",parts["image"])\nprint("Persistent:",parts["data"])',
 'Explain the difference between two containers created from one image using a Python equality check.',
 'a={"name":"web-a","image":"notes:42"}\nb={"name":"web-b","image":"notes:42"}\nprint("Same image:",a["image"]==b["image"])\nprint("Same instance:",a["name"]==b["name"])',
 [('Does rebuilding an image replace a running container automatically?','No','Always','Only if Python is installed','Recreate the container using the selected image.'),('Does a registry run the API?','No; it stores image artifacts','Yes; it is the web server','Yes; it is the database','Choose a runtime host separately.')],
 'An image is a running virtual machine.','source → build image → run container → attach external data','Packaging, execution and persistent state have separate lifecycles.',syntax='docker build -t image:tag .\ndocker run image:tag',shell='docker version\ndocker run --rm python:3.12-slim python -c "print(6 * 7)"')

L(0,'2. Run one process and understand its exit status','Read an entry command and distinguish a completed job from a long-running server.',
 [('Foreground process and exit status','A container normally lives while its main process lives. Exit status 0 means the process reported success; it does not promise that a long-lived service is available.'),('--rm and image download','--rm removes that disposable container when it exits, not its image. The first run may download an image; network access is needed until it is cached.')],
 'A one-line Python program can print 42 and exit successfully. Its container then stops because there is no web server left running. This is useful for a job and wrong for an API expected to accept requests. A server instead keeps an event loop listening until it is stopped.\n\nLearn the command shape before adding options: docker run [options] IMAGE COMMAND [arguments]. Everything after the image is the container command, not another host-shell command. Run each terminal line separately in PowerShell. The Python example inspects a completed, owned subprocess and its return code; it does not pretend to be Docker output.',
 'import subprocess,sys\nresult=subprocess.run([sys.executable,"-c","print(6*7)"],capture_output=True,text=True,check=True)\nprint("Output:",result.stdout.strip())\nprint("Exit:",result.returncode)',
 'Run a Python child that prints ready, and require a successful exit.',
 """import subprocess,sys
r=subprocess.run([sys.executable,"-c","print('ready')"],capture_output=True,text=True,check=True)
print(r.stdout.strip(),r.returncode)""",
 'Observe a deliberate nonzero exit without claiming it is a passing application test.',
 'import subprocess,sys\nr=subprocess.run([sys.executable,"-c","raise SystemExit(3)"],capture_output=True,text=True)\nprint("Reported failure:",r.returncode==3)',
 [('Why does a print-only container stop?','Its main process exited','The image was deleted','The registry timed out','A container is tied to its process lifecycle.'),('Does --rm delete the downloaded image?','No','Yes','Only for Python','It removes the disposable container instance.')],
 'docker run IMAGE; assume an API is listening forever.','Choose a command that keeps the intended service process alive.','A successful short job and an available long-running service are different outcomes.',shell='docker run --rm python:3.12-slim python -c "print(42)"')

L(0,'3. Files inside a container are not a data durability plan','Separate image files, a writable container layer and persistent storage.',
 [('Writable layer','Changes made inside one container belong to that container unless stored in an external mount. Recreating the container starts another writable layer.'),('Named volume and bind mount','A named volume is managed by Docker. A bind mount exposes a specific host path. Mounting over a directory can hide image files already at that path.')],
 'Your application source belongs in an image. Your database belongs in storage whose lifecycle survives a replacement. Writing notes into the image filesystem may appear to work until a deployment recreates the container. Restarting the same container and recreating a new one are not the same experiment.\n\nThe local Python example gives two independent temporary directories different files. It illustrates ownership without deleting any real folder. In the actual lab, PostgreSQL uses pg_data, a named volume. The API has a read-only root filesystem and receives a temporary /tmp mount. A volume keeps data beyond one container; it is still on one host and is not itself a backup.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nfrom tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nwith TemporaryDirectory() as first,TemporaryDirectory() as second:\n    Path(first,"note.txt").write_text("owned example",encoding="utf-8")\n    print("Original instance:",Path(first,"note.txt").exists())\n    print("Replacement instance:",Path(second,"note.txt").exists())',
 'Write and reopen one small fictional file in an explicitly owned temporary directory.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nfrom tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nwith TemporaryDirectory() as d:\n    p=Path(d)/"draft.txt";p.write_text("Practice",encoding="utf-8")\n    print(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))',
 'Find the persistent PostgreSQL mount in the supplied Compose file.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\nc=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())\nprint(c["services"]["db"]["volumes"][0])\nprint("API root read-only:",c["services"]["api"]["read_only"])',
 [('Is a named volume automatically an off-host backup?','No','Always','Only if called backup','It persists on its Docker host; independent recovery copies are separate.'),('Can a mount hide files from an image directory?','Yes','Never','Only on Windows','The mounted directory appears at that path.')],
 'Store the only database copy in a disposable API layer.','Keep database state in a persistent store and test independent backups.','Persistence survives replacement; backup supports recovery when the persistent store is lost.')

L(0,'4. Listening address, container port and published host port','Trace a request from your browser through a port mapping to a listening process.',
 [('0.0.0.0; loopback','0.0.0.0 means listen on all interfaces in the current network namespace. 127.0.0.1 is loopback for that namespace. Inside a container it is not the host.'),('Publish mapping; EXPOSE','A host mapping such as 127.0.0.1:8042:8002 sends host port 8042 to container port 8002. EXPOSE documents a container port but does not publish it.')],
 'Imagine the browser address, host mapping and server socket as three linked stops. Our browser opens host port 8042. Docker forwards that request into container port 8002. The Python process must listen on 0.0.0.0:8002 inside that container to accept traffic arriving at its network interface.\n\nKeeping the host side bound to 127.0.0.1 makes this local lab available only from the host, even though the container listens on all of its own interfaces. A phone cannot reach your laptop by using the phone’s localhost. Public phone testing happens later through an intentionally deployed HTTPS hostname. Do not expose the database just to make the API reachable.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\nc=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())\nhost,published,target=c["services"]["api"]["ports"][0].split(":")\nprint("Host address:",host)\nprint("Browser port:",published)\nprint("Container port:",target)',
 'Parse a fictional local mapping 127.0.0.1:9000:8000 and identify both ports.',
 'address,outer,inner="127.0.0.1:9000:8000".split(":")\nprint("Browser:",outer)\nprint("Server:",inner)',
 'Verify that PostgreSQL has no published host port in the supplied lab.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\ndb=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]["db"]\nprint("Database published:","ports" in db)',
 [('Does EXPOSE 8002 publish a host port?','No','Yes','Only for FastAPI','It is image metadata, not host port forwarding.'),('What does localhost mean inside the API container?','That container itself','The database service','Every device on Wi-Fi','Use the Compose service name to reach another service.')],
 'Connect to http://localhost:8002 on the phone and expect the laptop API.','Use the intended host mapping locally, or the deployed HTTPS hostname on the phone.','Every device and container has its own loopback address.',syntax='127.0.0.1:HOST_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT\nAPI process inside container: 0.0.0.0:CONTAINER_PORT')

L(0,'5. Start, inspect narrowly, stop gracefully and recreate','Choose lifecycle commands without confusing restart with a new release.',
 [('Detached mode and logs','-d runs a service in the background. Logs are process output; inspect only the owned service and never print full secret-bearing environment data.'),('SIGTERM and graceful shutdown','A normal stop requests termination and gives the process a grace period. The server should stop accepting new work, finish bounded work and release resources.')],
 'Use a deliberately named throwaway container so its lifecycle is easy to inspect. A print-only command stops immediately; docker ps without -a normally lists running containers, so it can look as if nothing happened. Inspect that one container’s state and read its own logs.\n\nFor the API lab, Compose owns service names and docker compose logs --tail 30 api is more focused than a system-wide dump. Restart runs the existing container again; recreating it uses selected image/configuration and a fresh writable layer. Our server uses an exec-form command and a finite graceful-shutdown timeout. Do not use forced termination as your normal release procedure.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("Dockerfile").read_text()\nprint("Exec-form start:",\'CMD ["python","server.py"]\' in text)\nprint("Non-root runtime:","USER 10001:10001" in text)',
 'Check that the server disables raw access logging and has a shutdown bound.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("server.py").read_text()\nprint("Access logging disabled:","access_log=False" in text or "access_log=False" in text.replace(" ",""))\nprint("Graceful timeout configured:","timeout_graceful_shutdown" in text)',
 'Model why restart leaves an image selection unchanged while a reviewed recreate can select another release.',
 'running={"image":"notes:release-a"}\nrestart=dict(running)\nreplacement={"image":"notes:release-b"}\nprint("Restart same image:",restart["image"]==running["image"])\nprint("Recreate selected new:",replacement["image"]!=running["image"])',
 [('Does restart select a newly built image automatically?','No','Always','Only with -d','Recreation is needed to use a new image selection.'),('Should full environment values be pasted into logs?','No','Yes for debugging','Only during deployment','Read narrowly scoped state and redact credentials.')],
 'Use forced kill as every normal deployment stop.','Use the normal stop/graceful shutdown path and verify bounded exit.','A deliberate lifecycle protects in-flight work and makes failures easier to explain.',shell='docker run --name week42-hello python:3.12-slim python -c "print(42)"\ndocker inspect --format "{{.State.Status}}" week42-hello\ndocker logs week42-hello\ndocker rm week42-hello')

L(0,'6. First checkpoint: prove the API contract before packaging','Carry a known-good health and authentication contract into the container week.',
 [('In-process contract check','TestClient exercises the API lifecycle and request handling without Docker or an open network port. It is a prerequisite check, not container evidence.'),('Verification boundary','A test can demonstrate its own boundary: local Python, application contract, image build, real database integration, or public HTTPS. Name the boundary instead of treating one green check as all of them.')],
 'Before changing packaging, establish that the API itself behaves as expected. The Week42 folder is a separate continuation of the Week41 Notes API: account registration, bearer authentication, owned notes, tags and structured telemetry remain. Its local examples use fresh SQLite state; its container stack uses PostgreSQL.\n\nThe helper practice creates owned temporary state and closes it afterwards. It is described in Week41 and included here. A 200 response from TestClient proves the route works under that fixture. It cannot prove a Docker port mapping, PostgreSQL role grant or public certificate. Those require the later integration and release checks. Keep this distinction while reading every output in this week.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,client,headers):\n    print("Live:",client.get("/health").json())\n    print("Ready:",client.get("/health/ready").json())\n    print("Anonymous notes:",client.get("/v1/notes").status_code)',
 'Use the isolated API client to check the public OpenAPI document.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.get("/openapi.json")\n    print(r.status_code)\n    print("Notes create route:","post" in r.json()["paths"]["/v1/notes"])',
 'Check that health returns a request correlation ID without inspecting any credential.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.get("/health")\n    print("Request id present:",bool(r.headers.get("x-request-id")))',
 [('Does TestClient prove a Docker image starts?','No','Yes','Only with a health endpoint','The container must also be built and run.'),('Where do these practice examples store their notes?','Owned temporary state','Your Study Hub browser progress','Your real GitHub account','The helper isolates every practice run.')],
 'A local 200 means the whole public deployment is proven.','Add evidence for packaging, real database, networking and public TLS separately.','Verification boundaries prevent a working component from being mistaken for a working deployment.')

L(1,'1. Read FROM, WORKDIR and dependency order','Explain the first half of the actual API Dockerfile.',
 [('Dockerfile and build context','A Dockerfile declares build instructions. The context is the set of files the builder can receive; COPY reads from it, not arbitrary host folders.'),('FROM; WORKDIR; COPY; RUN','FROM chooses a base, WORKDIR selects a working directory, COPY adds context files, and RUN executes a build-time command. RUN is not the service startup command.')],
 'Read the supplied Dockerfile from top to bottom. The builder starts from Python 3.12 slim, sets /build, copies requirements.txt and installs dependencies into /opt/venv. Application files are copied later in the runtime stage. That ordering makes a common source-only edit less likely to invalidate dependency installation.\n\nThe dot at the end of docker build is the context directory, not decoration. Run the command from docs/python-week42. Sending the repository root would add unrelated lessons and increase the chance of sending private files. The base minor-version tag is intentionally readable but mutable; a reviewed release later records an immutable digest and rebuild policy.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("Dockerfile").read_text()\nprint("Stages:",sum(line.startswith("FROM ") for line in text.splitlines()))\nprint("Requirements first:",text.index("COPY requirements.txt")<text.index("COPY orm_api"))',
 'Find the builder working directory and the runtime working directory.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path("Dockerfile").read_text().splitlines():\n    if line.startswith("WORKDIR"):print(line)',
 'Check that the image starts the server rather than installing dependencies each time it starts.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("Dockerfile").read_text()\ncmd=next(line for line in text.splitlines() if line.startswith("CMD "))\nprint(cmd)\nprint("Startup installs:","pip install" in cmd)',
 [('When does RUN execute?','At image build time','On every HTTP request','Only after a container stops','Service startup is configured separately.'),('What does the final dot in docker build mean?','The build context directory','The image version','The container hostname','Choose the intended project folder before building.')],
 'Build from any directory and assume COPY sees the intended files.','Build from the reviewed project context: docker build -t studyhub-week42-api:local .','The context boundary is part of both correctness and data exposure.',syntax='FROM python:3.12-slim AS builder\nWORKDIR /build\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN python -m venv /opt/venv',shell='docker build -t studyhub-week42-api:local .')

L(1,'2. Keep private files out with a context allowlist','Explain why .gitignore alone does not protect a Docker build.',
 [('.dockerignore','Controls what is sent in the build context. It is independent of .gitignore. A file ignored by Git can still enter a build unless excluded here.'),('Allowlist','This project starts with ** and then includes specific source paths. That makes unlisted runtime state, backups and local secret files stay outside the context.')],
 'The app needs source, migrations and installed libraries. It does not need your instance database, signing key, backups, .venv or local .secrets folder. A developer can keep a file untracked and still accidentally send it to a remote builder. Both version-control and build-context boundaries must be reviewed.\n\nOur Dockerfile also uses explicit COPY paths rather than COPY . . as a second clear boundary. The Python check below reads the authored allowlist; it does not implement Docker’s pattern matcher. The real build and image checks in CI provide additional evidence. Deleting a secret from a later image layer does not erase a copy embedded in an earlier layer.',
 'from pathlib import Path\npatterns=Path(".dockerignore").read_text().splitlines()\nprint("Deny by default:",patterns[0]=="**")\nprint("Source allowed:","!orm_api/**" in patterns)\nprint("Private folder allowed:","!.secrets/" in patterns)',
 'Confirm that the lab ignores private secret files in Git as well as excluding them from its build allowlist.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ngit=Path(".gitignore").read_text()\ndocker=Path(".dockerignore").read_text()\nprint("Git ignores local secrets:",".secrets/" in git)\nprint("Docker explicitly allows secrets:","!.secrets/" in docker)',
 'Check that the image copies application directories explicitly.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("Dockerfile").read_text()\nprint("Explicit API copy:","COPY orm_api ./orm_api" in text)\nprint("Copy everything:","COPY . ." in text)',
 [('Does .gitignore replace .dockerignore?','No','Yes','Only for private repos','They protect different boundaries.'),('Does a later deletion erase a secret from an older image layer?','No','Always','Only if the filename changes','Keep secrets outside the build from the beginning.')],
 'COPY . . and rely only on .gitignore.','Use a reviewed .dockerignore and explicit COPY paths.','Credentials should be provided at runtime, not embedded and then hidden.')

L(1,'3. Multi-stage builds and the matching runtime','Separate dependency installation from the final runtime stage.',
 [('Multi-stage build; COPY --from','Each FROM starts a stage. COPY --from takes a selected artifact from another stage without inheriting all of that stage’s other files.'),('Virtual environment compatibility','A copied virtual environment depends on interpreter, platform and system libraries. Build and runtime here use the same Python image family; arbitrary cross-platform copying is unsafe.')],
 'The builder installs runtime requirements into /opt/venv. The final stage copies that directory and the required source. Tests, developer tools, local caches and build working files are not copied just because they existed in a previous stage. This keeps the final artifact’s intent readable.\n\nMulti-stage does not magically shrink every application or remove vulnerable runtime libraries. Measure the final image, inspect its contents and keep dependencies patched. Copying a Windows .venv into a Linux image is not equivalent to installing Linux dependencies in the builder. For stricter repeatability, resolve and record the same reviewed base image digest for both stages.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nlines=Path("Dockerfile").read_text().splitlines()\nfor line in lines:\n    if line.startswith("FROM ") or line.startswith("COPY --from"):print(line)',
 'Verify that developer test dependencies are not installed by the Dockerfile.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("Dockerfile").read_text()\nprint("Runtime requirements installed:","-r requirements.txt" in text)\nprint("Dev requirements installed:","requirements-dev.txt" in text)',
 'Extract the shared base-image argument and explain whether the tag is immutable.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nline=next(x for x in Path("Dockerfile").read_text().splitlines() if x.startswith("ARG PYTHON_IMAGE="))\nprint(line)\nprint("Pinned by digest:","@sha256:" in line)',
 [('Can a Windows .venv be copied blindly into Linux?','No','Always','If it contains Python files','Binary extensions and interpreter paths depend on platform.'),('Does a mutable tag identify one permanent image?','No','Yes','Only for slim images','Record a digest when release identity must be immutable.')],
 'Assume multiple stages automatically prove a minimal secure image.','Inspect the final stage and verify actual runtime contents.','A build pattern supports a goal; measured artifact evidence supports the conclusion.',syntax='FROM BASE AS builder\n# install into /opt/venv\nFROM BASE AS runtime\nCOPY --from=builder /opt/venv /opt/venv')

L(1,'4. Run as a non-root user and grant only writable paths','Relate a container user, a read-only filesystem and mounted secrets.',
 [('USER and numeric UID/GID','The runtime process runs under the configured user. UID 10001 is an ordinary container account here, not the root account.'),('Read-only root filesystem; tmpfs','A read-only root stops ordinary writes to image paths. tmpfs gives temporary writable storage. Neither setting replaces application authentication or host security.')],
 'The Dockerfile creates an application account and switches to UID/GID 10001. Compose further makes the API root filesystem read-only and supplies /tmp for temporary work. PostgreSQL owns durable data elsewhere, so the API does not need to write its source directory.\n\nFile permissions still matter: a non-root process must be able to read explicitly granted secret mounts. The lab’s secret generator protects the host directory and creates readable bind-secret files without printing them. Local Compose file-backed secrets are mounts, not an encrypted secret vault, and UID/mode remapping has limitations. On a shared host or real provider, use that platform’s secret access controls and review its effective file permissions.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\nimage=Path("Dockerfile").read_text();api=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]["api"]\nprint("Ordinary UID:","USER 10001:10001" in image)\nprint("Read-only root:",api["read_only"])\nprint("Temporary writable mounts:",api["tmpfs"])',
 'Check that the API drops Linux capabilities and prevents gaining new privileges.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\na=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]["api"]\nprint(a["cap_drop"])\nprint(a["security_opt"])',
 'Create local secret fixtures twice and prove existing values were not replaced, without printing them.',
 'from tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom ops import init_secrets\nwith TemporaryDirectory() as d:\n    folder=Path(d)/"private"\n    first=init_secrets(folder);before={p.name:p.read_bytes() for p in folder.iterdir()}\n    print("First creation:",len(first))\n    print("Second creation:",len(init_secrets(folder)))\n    print("Preserved:",before=={p.name:p.read_bytes() for p in folder.iterdir()})',
 [('Does non-root replace API authorisation?','No','Yes','Only inside Docker','Operating-system and application boundaries solve different problems.'),('Are local Compose secret files automatically an encrypted vault?','No','Yes','Only on Linux','They are explicitly granted file mounts; review host/provider access.')],
 'Run the API as root just to avoid thinking about file ownership.','Use an ordinary runtime user and grant the required paths explicitly.','A correct permission model makes the application work without broad write authority.')

L(1,'5. Startup configuration is runtime configuration','Keep credentials out of build arguments and validate the start command.',
 [('Exec-form CMD','A JSON command array starts the program without an extra command shell. Signals and argument boundaries are easier to reason about.'),('Build-time ARG versus runtime configuration','ARG influences building; ENV provides image/container defaults. Neither is the right place to bake real credentials into an image. Runtime secret sources remain outside the artifact.')],
 'The image’s default command is python server.py. That entry point creates the app through a factory, validates the port and binds to the intended interface. The image can then be promoted between environments while receiving different runtime configuration.\n\nA production flag does not transform arbitrary code into a secure service. It makes this project require an external signing secret. It does not add HTTPS, a rate limiter, backups or an operations team. The code below checks rejected configuration using a fictional short string and reports only the fact of rejection. Never dump os.environ to diagnose a credential problem.',
 'from orm_api.settings import Settings\ntry:Settings.from_env({"APP_ENV":"production"})\nexcept ValueError:print("Missing production secret rejected")\nconfig=Settings.from_env({"APP_ENV":"production","API_JWT_SECRET":"fictional-only-"*4})\nprint("Mode:",config.environment)\nprint("Secret appears in repr:","fictional-only-" in repr(config))',
 'Verify that an invalid numeric port fails before a server starts.',
 'from orm_api.settings import Settings\ntry:Settings.from_env({"PORT":"not-a-port"})\nexcept ValueError:print("Invalid port rejected")',
 'Verify that a provider PORT value takes precedence over the local default.',
 'from orm_api.settings import Settings\nc=Settings.from_env({"PORT":"9000","API_PORT":"8002"})\nprint("Selected port:",c.port)',
 [('Should a real signing key be a Docker build ARG?','No','Yes','Only for a private image','Build artifacts/history are not a runtime secret boundary.'),('Does APP_ENV=production add HTTPS automatically?','No','Always','Only in a slim image','TLS is a separate deployment capability.')],
 'Bake a production credential into a Dockerfile ENV line.','Supply a validated runtime secret source and keep values out of diagnostics.','One reusable artifact should not contain environment-specific credentials.',syntax='CMD ["python","server.py"]\n# Runtime: APP_ENV, PORT, API_JWT_SECRET_FILE or provider secret value')

L(1,'6. Build evidence, tags and a repeatable review','Distinguish a source review from an image that has actually been built and run.',
 [('Image tag and digest','A tag is a convenient name that can move. A digest identifies image content. Source commit, image digest and deployment record answer related but different questions.'),('Build cache','A cache can reuse unchanged build results. A fast cached build is not evidence that fresh upstream base packages were checked.')],
 'Use a deliberate tag for this lab and review the Dockerfile plus context before building. Then build the actual image and start the real Compose stack in Day 3. Record the source commit and the resolved image identity when promoting a release. An old successful run or a static Dockerfile parser cannot substitute for executing the current artifact.\n\nThis course includes a GitHub workflow that runs Python checks, builds the image, starts PostgreSQL and exercises the API. Its exact-commit result is the evidence to inspect. A rebuild using --pull can refresh a mutable base tag; --no-cache changes layer reuse but does not itself promise a new base image. Rebuild deliberately, verify again and retain the reviewed release identity.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport hashlib\nsource=Path("Dockerfile").read_bytes()\nfingerprint=hashlib.sha256(source).hexdigest()\nprint("Dockerfile source fingerprint length:",len(fingerprint))\nprint("This is an OCI image digest:",False)',
 'Create a release record using fictional immutable image identity and source revision, without calling a host.',
 'release={"source_revision":"example-commit-42","image":"registry.example/notes@sha256:"+"a"*64,"verified":False}\nprint("Immutable reference:","@sha256:" in release["image"])\nprint("Verified yet:",release["verified"])',
 'Check that the actual workflow contains both Docker build and container smoke steps.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("container_smoke.py").read_text()\nprint("Checks runtime UID:","10001" in text)\nprint("Checks restored database:","studyhub_restore" in text)',
 [('Is a Dockerfile source hash the resulting image digest?','No','Always','Only for one-stage builds','The built artifact has its own content identity.'),('Does --no-cache alone force a new base image download?','No','Always','Only with Python','Use a deliberate base selection and pull policy.')],
 'Call a release verified because an earlier commit built successfully.','Check build and runtime evidence for the exact selected source/artifact.','Release evidence must describe what you are actually deploying.',shell='docker build --pull -t studyhub-week42-api:local .\ndocker image inspect --format "{{.Id}}" studyhub-week42-api:local')
